messages:
customer_ops: Hey team — Bitlane (prod) is getting repeated 429s from the EU cluster since 09:20 UTC. They say it started suddenly and affects both chat and embeddings endpoints. sample request ids: r-8f3a, r-8f3b, r-8f4c.

sam_support: Thanks — can you paste the raw response body or headers they saw? 429 with Retry-After? :thinking_face:

customer_ops: Payload shows HTTP/1.1 429 Too Many Requests, headers: `X-RateLimit-RPM: 600`, `X-RateLimit-TPM: 12000`, no Retry-After header. They claim they didn't change client config.

maria_sre: Curious — no Retry-After from gateway usually means we rejected earlier than token bucket refill. Are we returning our internal headers? can you share request patterns (burstiness)?

customer_ops: They sent a mix: hourly cron + user actions. Their graph shows big spikes at 09:20 and 09:50 UTC, short 10-15s bursts, ~800 req/s in spikes.

jordan_prod: @maria_sre I pulled the tenant metrics — Bitlane is on shared plan with 600 RPM/12k TPM hard limits (matches headers). At 09:20 we saw a 3x temporary increase in call concurrency, tokens/sec rose ~4x. Backend reported token queueing and several `429 gateway_throttle` counters.

maria_sre: That matches classic token-window exhaustion. Two immediate asks: 1) can we reproduce a minute-aligned fanout? 2) any recent deploys to edge or ratecap?

ankur_dev: No deploys in last 48h according to CI. Repro idea: their CRON jobs look like they fire at TTL-aligned times (top of 10-minute). If a lot of tenants do that, we get synchronized bursts. Suggest advising client to jitter or use progressive backoff.

sam_support: For triage, can we run a short rule change: temporarily relax per-tenant burst smoothing for Bitlane (10% bump) while we confirm? Ops, is that safe? :pray:

maria_sre: We can do a temporary burst allowance on the edge token-bucket for that tenant for 30m. But prefer to route to a compatible lower-throughput model as fallback if sustained. I'll open a short window now.

deploy-bot: deploy-started: feature/tenant-burst-bypass -> edge-ratecap (sha: 3a7c9d). Estimated rollout 30s.

jordan_prod: Change applied, using bypass flag for tenant:bitlane, TTL 30m. Monitoring now.

customer_ops: After the bypass, Bitlane reports error rate dropped to ~0.2% from ~6% and latency back to normal. They still see small spikes but OK for now.

maria_sre: Good. Root cause hypothesis: client cron + lack of jitter caused short RPM/TPM window saturation leading to token bucket depletion on the gateway. Action items: 1) recommend client add randomized jitter and exponential backoff (we can share code). 2) Add a default prefix KV cache for chat sessions to reduce token usage on repeat requests. 3) On our side, create an alert that watches per-tenant short-window RPM bursts vs sustained TPM to detect this pattern earlier.

sam_support: I'll draft a customer note with suggested retry/backoff snippet and offer a 24h safety bump if they need runway.

ankur_dev: Snippet for client jitter (example):
```
// javascript
const jitter = () => Math.random() * 1000;
async function sendWithBackoff(fn, attempts=0){
  try { return await fn(); }
  catch(e){
    if (attempts>5) throw e;
    await sleep((2**attempts)*100 + jitter());
    return sendWithBackoff(fn, attempts+1);
  }
}
```

customer_ops: Thanks all — passing to Bitlane now. Please keep the bypass window visible here. :thumbsup:

maria_sre: Noted. I'll follow up with a short postmortem and the alert runbook. Closing provisional until we see any regression.

sam_support: Closing in ~30m unless we see fresh 429s. :hourglass_flowing_sand:
